{% extends "../layout.njk" %}
{% block headStylesheets %}
<link rel="preload" as="style" href="/pages/learn-some-facts.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/pages/learn-some-facts.css">
{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<div class="text-center wrap head-banner">
    <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/Learn Some Facts - Banner.png">
    <p>
        What happens when medical information becomes something you can explore? This project
        uses visual and interactive elements to explore a subject that can often feel quite clinical and
        difficult to take in. Have a play below, and then scroll down to see my how and why.
    </p>
</div>
<div class="gallery top-gallery">
    <div class="gallery-item">
        <canvas id="canvas-2" width="300" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item">
        <canvas id="canvas-3" width="300" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item">
        <canvas id="canvas-4" width="300" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item text-center">
        <h2 class="starting-point">The Starting Point</h2>
        <svg class="yellow-svg" viewBox="0 0 938.22 708.35">
            <path d="M922,708c-288.6-2.5-577-5-865-7.5C37,478,18,256,0,33,312,22,625,11,938,0c-5,236-10,472-15,708Z" />
            <text x="100" y="100">When my mother was diagnosed with breast cancer, we</text>
            <text x="100" y="130">were overwhelmed, stressed and, honestly, just sad.</text>
            <text x="100" y="190">There's a seemingly endless supply of information and</text>
            <text x="100" y="220">people willing to help, but I found a lot of that information</text>
            <text x="100" y="250">difficult to take in. I was also surprised by how little I knew</text>
            <text x="100" y="280">about something that affects so many people.</text>
            <text x="100" y="340">Cancer isn't exactly a topic people want to explore until</text>
            <text x="100" y="370">they have a reason to. So I wanted to take something</text>
            <text x="100" y="400">quite serious and information-heavy and make it feel a</text>
            <text x="100" y="430">little more engaging. Rather than presenting the</text>
            <text x="100" y="460">information as a traditional article or infographic, I wanted</text>
            <text x="100" y="490">to create something that encouraged people to explore,</text>
            <text x="100" y="520">discover and learn at their own pace.</text>
        </svg>
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/What problem was i trying to solve.png" class="paper-tag">
    </div>
</div>
<div class="text-center wrap">
    <h2>The Idea</h2>
    <br />
    <p>
        So, one of the things that makes cancer frightening is that you often don't know it's there.
        <br /><br />
        Some cancers can develop without obvious symptoms, which is why things like regular checks and screening can be
        so important. I started thinking about this idea of something being hidden beneath the surface. That led to the
        main
        interaction: I wanted the user to have to search for the cancer.
        <br /><br />
        Rather than immediately showing the user where the cancer was, I made them explore the body using an x-ray. As
        the
        cursor moves around, the inside of the body is gradually revealed. Eventually, the user will discover the
        brightly
        coloured cancer hidden within it.
    </p>
</div>

<svg viewBox="0 0 2248.11 1988.17" class="black-svg">
    <path
        d="M2248.11,1988.17c-749.37-19.75-1498.74-39.5-2248.11-59.25C22.17,1301.19,44.34,673.46,66.51,45.73,774.61,30.49,1482.71,15.24,2190.81,0c19.1,662.72,38.2,1325.44,57.3,1988.17Z" />
</svg>

<div class="wrap visual-direction gallery">
    <div class="gallery-item-60">
        <h2>Visual Direction</h2><br />
        <p>
            I knew quite early on that I didn't want this to look like a typical medical
            website.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            A lot of medical information is presented in a very clean, clinical way,
            which makes sense, but I wanted to create something that felt a little
            more human and inviting. The subject is already quite heavy, so I
            wanted the visual style to encourage people to explore it rather than
            making it feel even more intimidating.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            I started playing with collage, torn paper, halftone textures and bold
            vector illustrations. The bright yellows, pinks and blues give the pages
            a sense of energy, while the darker x-ray imagery creates a strong
            contrast and keeps bringing the user back to the idea of looking
            beneath the surface.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            The colour also became a useful way of highlighting the important parts
            of the interaction. Against the muted x-ray, the cancer immediately
            stands out, making it feel like something you've discovered.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            Although each cancer has its own layout and colour emphasis, I kept
            the same visual ingredients throughout. This gave me enough freedom
            to make each page feel different, while still making it obvious that they
            belong to the same project.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item-40">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/Moodboard.png">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/Colour Pallet.png">
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item-25">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/lungs.png">
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item-25">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/type1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item-25">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/bones.png">
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item-25">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/cough.png">
    </div>
</div>

<div class="building-it">
    <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/Buildin it.png">
</div>
<div class="gallery">
    <div class="gallery-item building-it-text">
        <p>
            The three pages also became a progression in how I approached Rive.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            Breast cancer was the starting point. It was my first experience building a
            project like this in Rive, so I was mainly figuring out how the different
            pieces worked and getting comfortable with State Machines, listeners and
            interactive states.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            Bone cancer came next. I was able to take what I'd learned from the first
            page and start pushing the interactions further. I became more confident
            with the animation and started introducing more interactive elements.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            By the time I reached lung cancer, I was much more comfortable
            experimenting. I started using more complex interactions, including lists
            and data binding, and felt more confident designing the experience around
            what the user could do rather than just what they could see.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            Looking back at the three pages, you can see that progression quite
            clearly. The visual design becomes more confident, the interactions
            become more ambitious, and my understanding of Rive develops
            alongside the project.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            The learning curve was definitely one of the biggest challenges. I was
            already comfortable with design and animation, but Rive introduced a more
            technical way of thinking. State Machines, overlapping timelines, listeners
            and data binding all took some time to wrap my head around.
        </p><br />
        <p>
            That was also one of the most rewarding parts of the project. Each page
            gave me something new to learn, which I could carry into the next one and
            use to make the experience more ambitious.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery-item">
        <img id="triggers-and-booleans" src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/Triggers-and-booleans.png">
        <img src="/assets/projects/learn-some-facts/State-Machine.png">
    </div>
</div>

{% endblock %}

{% set riveInstances = [
"lung_cancer",
"bone_cancer",
"breast_cancer_v2"
] %}